{
  "title": "The 50% Trap",
  "duration": 180,
  "script": "Here is one hundred dollars. It drops by fifty percent. That leaves fifty. Then it gains fifty percent of fifty, adding twenty five. You land at seventy five. Most people expect one hundred again. But percentages don't work that way. The dashed line marks the missing twenty five. That is why a fifty percent gain cannot undo a fifty percent loss. Now watch the base shrink. Take the same one hundred dollars and split it into ten blocks of ten. Lose one block, and only ninety remain. To climb back, you need nine dollars. Nine dollars is ten percent of ninety, but only nine percent of the original hundred. So the recovery block is already narrower than the block that left. And every later percentage points at a different total. Ten percent of ninety is nine, but ten percent of one hundred is ten. The same percentage, a different amount, because the base changed. To recover fifty percent, you must double what is left. That is why the climb feels harder than the fall. This is the shrinking base. Recovery grows faster than the loss. Watch the ladder. A ten percent drop needs eleven point one percent to recover. A twenty five percent drop needs thirty three point three. A fifty percent drop needs one hundred. And an eighty percent drop needs four hundred percent. The drop is measured from the old base, but the recovery is measured from the new smaller base. The smaller the base, the larger the climb. That is why the gold bars stretch wider and wider as the loss deepens. At ten percent down the gap is small. At eighty percent down, you need five times your money to break even. Now two real world traps. First, a price tag. A hundred dollar item is twenty percent off, so you pay eighty. Then twenty percent tax is added. Tax is charged on eighty, not on the original hundred, so you pay ninety six. You never get back to one hundred. Second, a rollercoaster. Two five percent gains compound to one hundred ten point two five. But one fifty percent gain followed by a fifty percent loss leaves seventy five. Same two moves, different order, different base. The tax lands after the discount, on a smaller price. And each rollercoaster percentage is applied to whatever came before. So remember the rule. After a loss, the gain is always measured from a smaller base. A small loss can demand a huge recovery. Ten percent down needs eleven point one up. Fifty percent down needs one hundred up. Eighty percent down needs four hundred up. Losses and gains are not symmetric, even when the percentages look equal. Protect the base, and you protect the compound. That is the fifty percent trap. Keep the base in mind.",
